<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-timers-interrupts">
    <title>Timers and Interrupts</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Every program you have written so far stops dead while it waits.
            <c>delay_ms(1000)</c> produces an accurate one-second delay, but for
            that entire second the CPU can do nothing else — not read a sensor,
            not update a display, not notice a button.  This chapter introduces
            the two mechanisms that end the waiting.  A hardware
            <term>timer</term> is a circuit that counts on its own, in parallel
            with whatever the CPU is doing.  An <term>interrupt</term> is the
            hardware's way of tapping the CPU on the shoulder when something —
            a timer reaching its period, a button changing state — needs
            attention, without the CPU ever having to ask.  Together they are
            how embedded software becomes responsive and efficient at the same
            time.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Choose prescaler and auto-reload values that make TIM14 mark
                    off a desired period from the 12 MHz clock, respecting the
                    16-bit limit of each register.</li>
                <li>Initialize TIM14 for periodic timing and check its update
                    flag in the main loop without blocking.</li>
                <li>Read a status bit's clear-by-write convention from the
                    reference manual and clear any status flag correctly.</li>
                <li>Convert a polled timer into an interrupt-driven one: enable
                    the update interrupt, enable the timer's line in the NVIC,
                    and write an Interrupt Service Routine under its exact
                    vector-table name.</li>
                <li>Share state between an ISR and <c>main()</c> through a
                    <c>volatile</c> flag, and apply the two ISR rules: get in
                    and out fast, and never block.</li>
                <li>Configure an external (GPIO) interrupt from a pushbutton
                    through the EXTI controller.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-timers-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: Timers and Interrupts</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This reading introduces two ideas.  The first is a piece of
                hardware: a counter that runs entirely on its own, which you
                configure once and then leave alone.  The second is the idea
                of an <term>interrupt</term>: hardware that can tap the CPU on
                the shoulder.  The reading stays at the level of ideas on
                purpose — the machinery behind them is the
                <term>pre-class video</term>, which walks the moving pictures
                a page of prose cannot, and the full written detail waits in
                this chapter's Reference section for whenever you want to
                revisit it.  Read this, watch the video, answer the questions
                (they cover both), and in class you will build the real
                thing: Blinky without a single call to <c>delay_ms()</c>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-cost-of-waiting">
            <title>The Cost of Waiting</title>
            <p>
                Waiting has been built into every program in this course so far.
                On Day 1 the delay was a <term>busy-wait loop</term> — a
                <c>for</c> loop that burned processor cycles doing nothing
                and on Day 2 you replaced it with the course's
                <c>delay_ms()</c>, which fixed the busy-wait's imprecision
                (<xref ref="subsec-day2-delay-ms"/>).  What <c>delay_ms()</c>
                did <em>not</em> fix is the captivity: while it runs, the CPU
                belongs to the delay.  A program that calls
                <c>delay_ms(1000)</c> is deaf and blind for one full second.
            </p>
            <p>
                You have felt this cost.  In the Lab 2 button-race game, the
                countdown flashes were produced by delays — and while the code
                sat inside a delay, it could not watch the buttons, so a press
                during the countdown could slip past unregistered.  The polling
                loops of Days 3 and 4 have the mirror-image problem: the CPU is
                fully awake, but it spends its time asking <q>anything yet?</q>
                over and over, and every additional job added to the loop makes
                every other job's response slower.
            </p>
            <p>
                What we actually want is to separate <em>keeping time</em> from
                <em>doing work</em>.  Let a dedicated piece of hardware count
                the milliseconds; let the CPU compute, print, and respond; and
                give the hardware a way to tell the CPU when the time has come.
                The first half of that bargain is the hardware timer.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timer-hardware">
            <title>A Circuit Whose Whole Job Is Counting</title>
            <p>
                A <term>hardware timer</term> is a small digital circuit inside
                the STM32C031C6, separate from the CPU, built around one job:
                counting clock pulses.  At its core (<xref ref="fig-counter-block"/>)
                it is a register that stores the current count and an adder that
                increments it; on each tick of its input clock, the stored count
                is replaced by the count plus one.  Once started, it counts
                whether the CPU is computing, sitting in a delay, or doing
                nothing at all.  The timer (also called counter) needs no attention to keep running:
                it runs entirely in hardware.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-counter-block">
                <caption>The core of a counter: a register holds the current
                count <m>n</m>; multiplexers select the next value (<m>n+1</m>,
                <m>n-1</m>, or 0); the register updates on the rising edge of
                its clock.  The slash marks indicate multi-wire (bus)
                connections.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/slide04_9200d3ec.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The STM32C031C6 has five of these timers — TIM1, TIM3, TIM14,
                TIM16, and TIM17 — all built around 16-bit counters, each
                independently configurable.  They range from simple to
                elaborate: TIM14 is the simplest, and it is the one this
                chapter uses.  The fancier ones can also measure the width of
                input pulses and generate output waveforms with no CPU
                involvement at all; those abilities return in the motor
                chapters.
            </p>
            <p>
                Two registers turn a bare counter into a programmable timer.
                The <term>prescaler</term> (PSC) divides the timer's input
                clock down to a chosen counting rate.  The
                <term>auto-reload register</term> (ARR) holds the count at
                which the timer wraps: the counter (CNT) runs 0, 1, 2,
                <ellipsis/> up to the auto-reload value, then resets to 0 and
                signals that a period has elapsed.  That signal is called the
                <term>update event</term>.  Rate, range, wrap — that is the
                whole machine.  (The chip's full block diagram, with much
                more bolted around this core, is in
                <xref ref="subsec-timers-ref-block"/> when you want it.)
            </p>
            <p>
                The update event leaves a footprint your code can see: the
                hardware sets the <term>update interrupt flag</term> (UIF), a
                bit in the timer's status register TIM14_SR.  The division of
                labor mirrors what you saw with the ADC on Day 7: the hardware
                sets the flag; noticing it — and then clearing it so the next
                period can be seen — is your code's job.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you have used Arduino's <c>millis()</c>, one adjustment
                before going on: <c>millis()</c> reads a free-running counter
                fed by a timer you never configured.  This chapter hands you
                exactly what <c>millis()</c> hides — choosing the count rate,
                noticing the wrap, and clearing the flag yourself.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timer-arithmetic">
            <title>How Fast and How Far to Count</title>
            <p>
                Configuring a timer means answering two questions: how fast
                should it count, and how far?  You do not program the timer
                with an amount of time — you program it with a counting rate
                (the prescaler) and a count (the auto-reload value), and the
                period follows from the arithmetic.
            </p>
            <p>
                Start from the clock.  The STM32C031C6 runs at 12 MHz, so a
                desired period converts to clock cycles as
                <m>\text{cycles} = f_{clock} \times t</m>.  A one-second period
                is <m>12{,}000{,}000</m> cycles — and there is the problem: the
                counter and the auto-reload register are 16 bits wide, so each
                holds <m>2^{16} = 65{,}536</m> distinct values, numbered 0
                through 65,535.  Twelve million does
                not fit.
            </p>
            <p>
                The prescaler is the fix.  It sits between the clock and the
                counter and divides the clock rate by any integer from 1 to
                65,536, so the counter can count <em>slower</em> instead of
                <em>farther</em>.  With the prescaler dividing by 12,000, the
                counter ticks at <m>12{,}000{,}000 / 12{,}000 = 1000</m> ticks
                per second — one tick per millisecond — and one second is a
                count of just 1000, which fits easily.  The period comes from
                the product: <m>\text{period} = (\text{prescale factor} \times
                \text{count}) / f_{clock}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                One convention will trip you if you are not warned: <em>both</em>
                registers are written as the desired value minus one since both 
                start counting from zero (the prescaler works by counting also).  The
                prescaler divides by <m>\text{PSC} + 1</m>, and the counter
                counts from 0 to ARR <em>inclusive</em> — 0 through 499 is 500
                ticks.  So a divide-by-12,000 prescale is written as
                <c>11999</c>, and a count of 1000 is written as <c>999</c>.
                The worked example, in full:
            </p>
            <p>
                <em>One update event per second, from a 12 MHz clock:</em>
                write <m>\text{PSC} = 12{,}000 - 1</m>, so the counter ticks at
                1 kHz; write <m>\text{ARR} = 1000 - 1</m>, so the counter wraps
                every 1000 ticks.  Check: <m>12{,}000 \times 1000 =
                12{,}000{,}000</m> cycles — exactly one second.  Exactly, that
                is, in <em>counts</em>: the 12 MHz clock derives from the chip's
                HSI48 RC oscillator, which the datasheet (Table 41) specifies
                at 47.92 to 48.40 MHz over temperature, so the arithmetic is
                exact and the second itself is good to about one percent.
            </p>
            <p>
                One more thing to bring: the prescaler gives you freedom.
                Many (prescale, count) pairs produce the same period, as long
                as the product is right and both values fit in 16 bits.  In
                class you will choose among such pairs for a different
                period — and walk the hardware's own timing diagram step by
                step — so bring the arithmetic, not just the answer.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-interrupt-idea">
            <title>The Idea of an Interrupt</title>
            <p>
                A timer counting on its own solves half the problem.  The other
                half is delivery: how does the CPU learn that the period has
                elapsed, without sitting in a loop asking?  Consider two ways
                of handling a question you have sent your instructor by email.
                You could stare at your inbox until the reply arrives — that is
                a blocking wait.  You could check your inbox after every
                paragraph of work — that is polling, and both of these cost
                you attention.  Or you could simply keep working until your
                mail program pings you.  The ping is the interrupt, and the
                whole point is what it lets you do the rest of the time.
            </p>
            <p>
                Here is the idea — and for now, only the idea.  An
                <term>interrupt</term> is a signal from a piece of hardware —
                a timer, a button's pin, the UART — that makes the CPU pause
                what it is doing, run a function you wrote, and then resume
                exactly where it left off, as if nothing had happened.  The
                function has a name of its own: an <term>Interrupt Service
                Routine</term>, or ISR.  The interruption can land between
                any two instructions — even in the middle of
                <c>delay_ms()</c> — and that is precisely what makes it
                powerful: your main program no longer has to watch for
                anything.  The hardware watches; your ISR responds;
                <c>main()</c> works.
            </p>
            <p>
                One caution about that acronym before it bites.  Since Day 5
                you have used <q>ISR</q> for a peripheral's <em>interrupt and
                status register</em> — <c>USART2-&gt;ISR</c>,
                <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR</c>.  The Interrupt <em>Service Routine</em>
                is a different thing wearing the same letters: not a register
                but a function you write.  In class you will also hear
                <q>handler</q> for the function, which is unambiguous.
            </p>
            <p>
                Two habits follow from the idea, and class makes them
                concrete.  Because an ISR borrows the CPU from your main
                program, it must be quick — do the minimum and get out.  And
                because the hardware, not your code, is the caller, hooking
                an ISR up means telling the hardware about it — which is most
                of what Day 8's class hour builds.  <em>How</em> the
                machinery works — what the CPU saves, how it finds your
                function, and why shared variables need a keyword called
                <c>volatile</c> — is the <term>pre-class video</term>; the
                written version lives in
                <xref ref="subsec-timers-ref-mechanism"/> and
                <xref ref="subsec-timers-ref-isr"/> for revisiting.
            </p>
        </subsection>

                <reading-questions xml:id="rq-timers-interrupts">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-busywait-clock">
                <statement><p>On Day 1 you produced a delay with a busy-wait
                loop, tuned so that it takes approximately 10 ms with the
                12 MHz clock.  The clock is later changed to 48 MHz.  What
                happens to the delay?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>It shrinks to approximately 2.5 ms —
                        four times shorter — because the same number of
                        iterations now executes four times faster.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  A busy-wait delay is tied to
                        the clock speed.  A hardware timer's period comes from
                        a programmed prescaler and count, so it is set
                        deliberately rather than inherited from however fast
                        the loop happens to run.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It stays at 10 ms because the compiler
                        adjusts the loop count for the new clock.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The compiler has no idea the loop is
                        meant to be a delay — it just generates instructions.
                        Nothing adjusts the iteration count when the clock
                        changes.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It grows to approximately 40 ms because
                        a faster clock consumes more power and slows
                        execution.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A faster clock makes the CPU run faster,
                        not slower — the same iterations complete in less
                        time, so the delay gets shorter.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It is unchanged because loops take the
                        same wall-clock time regardless of clock speed.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Loop iterations are made of instructions,
                        and instructions execute at the clock rate.  Quadruple
                        the clock and the same loop finishes in a quarter of
                        the time.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-timer-period">
                <statement><p>A timer's input clock runs at 1 MHz after
                prescaling.  The auto-reload register (ARR) is set to 499.
                How often does the timer generate an update event?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Every 500 µs — the counter increments
                        every 1 µs and wraps after 500 ticks (0 through
                        499).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The counter counts 0, 1, 2,
                        <ellipsis/> 499, then wraps — 500 ticks at 1 µs each,
                        so 500 µs per period.  The written value is always the
                        desired count minus one.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Every 499 µs — the counter counts from
                        1 to 499.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The counter starts at 0 and counts to ARR
                        <em>inclusive</em>: that is ARR + 1 = 500 ticks per
                        period, not 499.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Every 1 ms — the timer always generates
                        events at 1 kHz regardless of ARR.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The event rate depends on both the
                        prescaled clock and ARR.  Here 500 ticks at 1 MHz give
                        500 µs.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Every 1 µs — the timer fires on every
                        clock tick.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The update event fires only when the
                        counter wraps at ARR.  With ARR = 499 that is once per
                        500 ticks — 500 µs.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-interrupt-vs-polling">
                <statement><p>A program must keep a display updating smoothly
                and also react to a sensor event that occurs at unpredictable
                times.  Compared with polling the sensor's status flag in the
                main loop, what does using an interrupt change?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The hardware notifies the CPU when the
                        event occurs, so the main loop spends no time checking
                        for it; the ISR runs briefly, then the main program
                        resumes where it left off.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The responsibility to notify
                        moves from the software (asking repeatedly) to the
                        hardware (announcing once).  The saved cycles can go
                        to real work — or the CPU can sleep, which is how
                        battery-powered devices live for months.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The ISR runs at the same time as the
                        main loop, on separate hardware, so the program does
                        two things at once.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The STM32C031C6 has one Cortex-M0+ core.
                        An ISR does not run beside the main program — it
                        <em>suspends</em> it, runs, and returns.  The gain is
                        that the suspension happens only when the event
                        actually occurs.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The sensor event itself is detected by
                        the hardware sooner, so the event happens
                        earlier.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>An interrupt changes how the CPU finds
                        out about the event, not when the event occurs.  What
                        improves is the response — the CPU no longer waits
                        until its next trip around the polling loop.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The event flag no longer needs to be
                        cleared, because the interrupt consumes it
                        automatically.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The flag still must be cleared — in the
                        ISR itself — or the interrupt fires again the moment
                        the ISR returns.  Interrupts change who notices the
                        flag, not whose job it is to lower it.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-isr-short">
                <statement><p>Why should an Interrupt Service Routine be kept
                as short as possible?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>While the ISR runs, the main program is
                        suspended and other interrupts can be kept waiting; a
                        slow ISR delays everything else the system must
                        do.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The standard pattern is to set
                        a <c>volatile</c> flag in the ISR and let the main
                        loop do the time-consuming work when it sees the
                        flag.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>ISRs have a hardware-enforced maximum
                        execution time and are terminated if they exceed
                        it.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The Cortex-M0+ imposes no time limit on
                        an ISR.  An ISR that never returns simply hangs the
                        rest of the program — nothing terminates it.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Long ISRs overflow the CPU's register
                        save area and corrupt the main program's
                        state.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The state saved at interrupt entry is
                        restored at exit regardless of how long the ISR ran.
                        The cost of a long ISR is blocked responsiveness, not
                        corruption.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The compiler cannot optimize code inside
                        an ISR, so long ISRs waste memory.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The compiler optimizes ISR code like any
                        other (subject to <c>volatile</c> on shared
                        variables).  The reason to keep ISRs short is
                        responsiveness.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-volatile">
                <statement><p>A global variable <c>uint8_t count</c> is
                incremented in an ISR and printed in <c>main()</c>.  The
                program prints 0 forever even though the ISR really does run
                every second.  What is the most likely cause?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p><c>count</c> is not declared
                        <c>volatile</c>, so the compiler assumes it never
                        changes inside <c>main()</c>'s loop and does not
                        re-read it from memory.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The ISR's write happens outside
                        the compiler's view of the loop.  <c>volatile</c>
                        tells the compiler to fetch the value fresh at every
                        use.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The ISR's name is misspelled.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A misspelled handler name would mean your
                        function is never wired into the vector table and so
                        never runs at all — but the problem states the ISR
                        runs every second.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The interrupt was never enabled in the
                        NVIC.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>With the NVIC line disabled the ISR would
                        never run — but the problem states it runs.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The timer's prescaler is set
                        incorrectly.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A wrong prescaler changes <em>how
                        often</em> the ISR runs, not whether <c>main()</c> can
                        see the variable it updates.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>

        <!--
        CANVAS QUIZ: "Before class - Timers and Interrupts"
        (Due 1 hour before class; auto-graded multiple choice; low stakes.)

        Q1. What is the main disadvantage of producing a delay with a busy-wait
            loop or delay_ms() in embedded software?
            A) The delay is always too long.
            B) The CPU is fully occupied for the whole delay and cannot respond
               to sensors, buttons, or other events during it.  <- CORRECT
            C) Delay loops consume more memory than hardware timers.
            D) The compiler removes delay loops when optimizing.

        Q2. A hardware timer's prescaler divides the 12 MHz system clock by
            12,000, and ARR is written as 999. What is the period between
            update events?
            A) 1 us
            B) 1 ms
            C) 1 s  <- CORRECT  (12 MHz / 12,000 = 1 kHz; 1000 ticks at 1 kHz = 1 s)
            D) 1 min

        Q3. When an interrupt fires, what does the CPU do before running the ISR?
            A) It finishes all pending work in main() first.
            B) It immediately jumps to the ISR, discarding the current instruction.
            C) It finishes the current instruction, pushes the machine state onto
               the stack, and loads the ISR's address from the vector table.  <- CORRECT
            D) It resets all registers to zero.

        Q4. An ISR sets a flag variable each time a timer fires; the main loop
            checks the flag and updates a display when it is set. Why is this
            preferred over updating the display inside the ISR?
            A) Displays cannot be written from inside an ISR.
            B) It keeps the ISR short, so the main program and other interrupts
               are not held up by slow work.  <- CORRECT
            C) Flag variables are faster to access than peripheral registers.
            D) Updating a display inside an ISR corrupts the stack.

        Q5. A variable is shared between an ISR and the main loop. What keyword
            must its declaration carry, and why?
            A) static - so it retains its value between ISR calls.
            B) const - so the ISR cannot accidentally change it.
            C) volatile - so the compiler re-reads it from memory at every use
               instead of assuming it never changes.  <- CORRECT
            D) extern - so it is visible across source files.
        -->
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-timers-day8">
        <title>Day 8 In-Class: Blinky Without Waiting</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today Blinky sheds its last delay.  You will configure TIM14 to
                mark off 500 ms periods entirely in hardware, first checking
                its flag from the main loop, then — the day's goal — letting
                the timer interrupt the CPU so that not even the checking
                remains.  By the end of the hour, your LED blinks from an ISR
                you wrote, and your main loop is free.
            </p>
            <p>
                One reassurance up front: nothing you can do today damages the
                board.  There is no wiring — the only hardware is the on-board
                LED — and the worst outcome of any mistake is a program that
                freezes or an LED that stays dark.  Both are cured by fixing
                the code and re-flashing.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-why">
            <title>Part 1: Why a Background Timer</title>
            <p>
                The case for the hardware timer is the case against captivity.
                While <c>ADCPot.c</c> sits in its <c>delay_ms(1000)</c>, its
                CPU idles through 12,000 clock cycles every millisecond; while
                the Lab 2 game flashed its countdown, button presses could
                slip past unwatched.  A timer that counts in hardware turns
                the relationship around: timekeeping costs the CPU nothing,
                and the CPU's whole attention is available for work — sampling
                a sensor at fixed intervals, timestamping events, running a
                control loop at a steady rate.  Next week the same timers will
                hold output pins high for precisely controlled durations —
                that is how motors get their speed commands — and measure how
                long input pulses last.  Today builds the foundation all of
                that stands on.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-captivity">
    <p><term>delay_ms() is accurate &#8212; and it holds the CPU hostage.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>While <c>delay_ms(1000)</c> runs, the CPU does nothing else for a full second: no sensor reads, no button checks, no printing.</li>
        <li>You felt this in the Lab 2 race game: presses during the countdown delays could slip past unwatched.</li>
        <li>The fix is separation: <term>hardware counts the time; the CPU does the work</term>. Configure TIM14 once and it counts on its own.</li>
        <li>Same machinery next week: timers will hold pins high for exact durations (motor speed) and measure pulse widths (the tachometer).</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Timekeeping moves to hardware; the CPU's attention comes back.</caption>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-design">
            <title>Part 2: Designing the 500 ms Timer</title>
            <p>
                The reading worked the arithmetic for a one-second period:
                cycles needed <m>= f_{clock} \times t</m>, split into a
                prescale factor and a count whose product hits the target,
                both minus-one when written (<xref ref="subsec-timer-arithmetic"/>).
                Today's timer toggles the LED every 500 ms, which at 12 MHz is
                <m>6{,}000{,}000</m> cycles.  Before any code, decide what to
                put in the two registers.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-arithmetic">
    <p><term>From the reading: rate &#215; count = period.</term> The worked one-second example:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
cycles needed = 12,000,000 cycles/s  x  1 s  =  12,000,000    // too big for 16 bits!

prescale by 12,000   ->  counter ticks at 1000 per second
count to 1000        ->  1000 ticks = 1 s        check: 12,000 x 1000 = 12,000,000
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Both registers are 16 bits: nothing written may exceed 65,535.</li>
        <li>Both are written <term>minus one</term>: divide-by-12,000 is written 11999; count-to-1000 is written 999. Counting starts at 0.</li>
        <li>Today's target is 500 ms &#8212; 6,000,000 cycles. Your turn.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>The reading's example. Today's period is different — the method is not.</caption>
</slide>

            <p>
                Before choosing values, watch the machine run.  The reference
                manual's own timing diagram for the prescaler tells the whole
                story, one stage at a time: the clock arrives and the counter
                starts; the counter reaches the auto-reload value, wraps to
                0, and the update event fires; a new prescaler value written
                mid-period waits in a buffer; and at the next update event it
                takes effect and the counter clock slows.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-prescaler-r1">
                <caption>The prescaler's timing diagram (RM0490 Figure 167),
                first stage: the input clock CK_PSC runs; when CEN (counter
                enable) is set, the counter clock CK_CNT starts.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/prescaler_reveal1.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-prescaler-r2">
                <caption>Second stage: the counter register climbs (F7, F8,
                <ellipsis/> FC here), reaches the auto-reload value, wraps to
                00 — and at that instant the update event (UEV) pulse
                fires.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/prescaler_reveal2.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-prescaler-r3">
                <caption>Third stage: software writes a new value into
                TIMx_PSC mid-period.  The prescaler control register shows
                the write immediately, but the working copy — the prescaler
                buffer — holds the old value until the next update
                event.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/prescaler_reveal3.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-prescaler-timing">
                <caption>The complete diagram.  At the update event the
                buffered prescaler value takes effect: the prescaler counter
                begins cycling 0<ndash/>3 and CK_CNT visibly slows — the
                division has changed from 1 to 4.  A new prescaler value
                never tears a period in half; it waits for the
                boundary.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/tim14_prescaler_timing.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-psc-r1" ref="fig-prescaler-r1">
    <caption>Clock in, enable on — the counter clock starts.</caption>
    <note>REVEAL sequence, four slides — advance as you narrate. Compressible: jump straight to the final stage if Part 2 is running long.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-psc-r2" ref="fig-prescaler-r2">
    <caption>The counter wraps at the auto-reload value — that pulse is the update event.</caption>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-psc-r3" ref="fig-prescaler-r3">
    <caption>A mid-period write to PSC lands in a buffer — nothing changes yet.</caption>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-psc-full" ref="fig-prescaler-timing">
    <caption>At the update event the new prescale takes effect — CK_CNT slows by 4. Periods never tear.</caption>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-timer-design">
                <title>Pick the Pair</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Four candidate (prescale factor, count) pairs for
                        6,000,000 cycles.  For each, decide: does it work?
                        Commit an answer for all four before we discuss.
                        Remember what must be true: the product must be
                        6,000,000, the count must be a whole number, and both
                        written values must fit in 16 bits (at most 65,536 as
                        a factor, written as 65,535).
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-design-t1">
                    <statement><p>Prescale by 10,000, count to 600.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-design-t2">
                    <statement><p>Prescale by 6000, count to 1000.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-design-t3">
                    <statement><p>Prescale by 60, count to 100,000.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-design-t4">
                    <statement><p>Prescale by 65,536, count to
                    91.55<ellipsis/></p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                The first two work — <m>10{,}000 \times 600</m> and
                <m>6000 \times 1000</m> are both exactly 6,000,000, and every
                value fits.  The third fails because 100,000 does not fit in a
                16-bit counter; the fourth fails because a counter cannot
                count to a fraction.  The course code uses a third working
                pair: prescale by 12,000 and count to 500.  It earns its place
                by making the counter tick at exactly 1 kHz — <em>one tick per
                millisecond</em> — so the count <em>is</em> the period in
                milliseconds, and changing the blink rate later means changing
                one self-explanatory number.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-design-reveal">
    <p><term>Which pairs make 6,000,000 cycles?</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>10,000 &#215; 600 = 6,000,000 &#8212; <clr c="green">works</clr></li>
        <li>6000 &#215; 1000 = 6,000,000 &#8212; <clr c="green">works</clr></li>
        <li>60 &#215; 100,000 &#8212; <clr c="red">fails</clr>: 100,000 does not fit in a 16-bit counter (65,535 max)</li>
        <li>65,536 &#215; 91.55&#8230; &#8212; <clr c="red">fails</clr>: a counter cannot count to a fraction</li>
        <li>The course code uses a third working pair, <term>12,000 &#215; 500</term>: the counter ticks exactly once per millisecond, so <term>the count is the period in ms</term> &#8212; change the blink rate later by changing one readable number.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Many pairs work; choose the one that keeps the arithmetic readable.</caption>
    <note>OPTIONAL / REVEAL — show after the activity.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-init">
            <title>Part 3: Five Lines of Initialization</title>
            <p>
                Turning the (prescale, count) pair chosen in
                <xref ref="subsec-day8-design"/> into a running timer takes
                five register
                writes, and every one of them is a pattern you have used
                before.  Here they are, one at a time.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>The clock.</term>  Like every peripheral, TIM14 is
                unreachable until its clock is enabled.  All five timers live
                on the APB bus.  TIM1, TIM14, TIM16, and TIM17 are enabled in
                <c>RCC_APBENR2</c> — the same register where you enabled the
                ADC's clock on Day 7; TIM3's enable bit is one register over,
                in <c>RCC_APBENR1</c>:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;   // enable clock access to TIM14
]]></code></program>
            <figure xml:id="fig-apbenr2-tim14">
                <caption>The RCC peripheral-clock-enable registers, with
                TIM14EN boxed in <c>RCC_APBENR2</c> (offset 0x40).  Familiar
                neighbors are visible: <c>RCC_IOPENR</c>, where the GPIO port
                clocks are enabled, sits at 0x34, and ADCEN — Day 7's clock
                enable — lives in the same APBENR2 row.  Until TIM14EN is
                set, TIM14's registers do not respond.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/apbenr2_tim14en.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <term>The rate and the range.</term>  The prescaler and
                auto-reload values from Part 2 go into <c>PSC</c> and
                <c>ARR</c>, each written as its value minus one:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define PSC_FACTOR 12000   // 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz: one tick per ms
#define ARR_FACTOR 500     // 500 ticks = 500 ms per update event

TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;   // counting starts at 0, so minus one
TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
]]></code></program>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-psc-arr">
                <caption>Three TIM14 registers from the register map: the
                counter itself (CNT, offset 0x24), the prescaler (PSC, 0x28),
                and the auto-reload value (ARR, 0x2C), each 16 bits.  The
                hardware divides by PSC + 1 and counts from 0 through ARR, so
                both PSC and ARR are written as the desired factor minus
                one.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/slide15_9029797c.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <term>A clean start.</term>  The counter register CNT holds
                whatever it held before — starting a 500-count period from a
                leftover 437 would make the first blink arrive early.  Zero it:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
TIM14->CNT = 0;   // don't start counting from a leftover value
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                <term>Go.</term>  The CEN bit (counter enable) in the control
                register <c>TIM14_CR1</c> starts the counting.  From this line
                on, the timer runs by itself:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;   // start the counter
]]></code></program>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-regmap-polled">
                <caption>An excerpt of the TIM14 register map (RM0490 Table 73) with today's
                two key bits marked: CEN in <c>TIM14_CR1</c> starts the
                counter, and UIF in <c>TIM14_SR</c> is set by hardware each
                time the counter wraps to 0 after reaching the auto-reload
                value — and is your responsibility to clear.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/tim14_regmap_polled.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Assembled in order — clock, rate, range, clean start, go — the
                five lines make the day's first new function:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_500ms_init() {
    // enable clock access to timer 14 (on APB bus)
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    // Set prescaler value
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;   // starts counting at 0
    // Set auto-reload value
    TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
    // Clear counter
    TIM14->CNT = 0;
    // Enable timer
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-reg-clock" ref="fig-apbenr2-tim14">
    <p><term>1. The clock.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><c>RCC-&gt;APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;</c></li>
        <li>Like every peripheral, TIM14 is unreachable until its clock is enabled — before this line, TIM14's registers do not respond.</li>
        <li>Same register as the ADC's clock enable on Day 7 &#8212; all five timers live on the APB bus.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-reg-pscarr" ref="fig-tim14-psc-arr">
    <p><term>2, 3, 4. Rate, range, clean start.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><c>TIM14-&gt;PSC = 12000 - 1;</c> &#8212; the counter ticks at 1 kHz: one tick per millisecond.</li>
        <li><c>TIM14-&gt;ARR = 500 - 1;</c> &#8212; wrap every 500 ticks: 500 ms per update event.</li>
        <li>Minus one, both times: the divider is PSC + 1, and counting runs 0 through ARR <em>inclusive</em>.</li>
        <li><c>TIM14-&gt;CNT = 0;</c> &#8212; never start a period from a leftover count.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-regmap" ref="fig-tim14-regmap-polled">
    <caption>CEN starts the counter; UIF rises each time it wraps. Clearing UIF is your job.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-init-code">
    <p><term>5. Go.</term> Assembled in order &#8212; clock, rate, range, clean start, enable:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_500ms_init() {
    // enable clock access to timer 14 (on APB bus)
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    // Set prescaler value
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;   // starts counting at 0
    // Set auto-reload value
    TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
    // Clear counter
    TIM14->CNT = 0;
    // Enable timer
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>Five writes, run once. After this, the timer needs nothing from the CPU.</caption>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-polled">
            <title>Part 4: Running blinkyTimerPolled.c</title>
            <p>
                The timer is now counting — the remaining question is how the
                program notices each elapsed period.  The first answer reuses
                what you know: check a status flag.  When the counter wraps,
                hardware sets UIF in <c>TIM14_SR</c>; the main loop checks for
                it, and on finding it set, clears it and toggles the LED.
            </p>
            <p>
                Look closely at the shape of that check, because it is
                deliberately <em>not</em> the wait you used on Days 5 and 7:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (!(ADC1->ISR & ADC_ISR_EOC)) {}   // Day 7: BLOCKING -- stand here until done

if (TIM14->SR & TIM_SR_UIF) { ... }     // today: check once, keep moving
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The <c>while</c> wait plants the program at the flag until it
                rises.  Today's <c>if</c> glances at the flag once per trip
                around the loop and moves on either way — which is the whole
                point: the rest of the loop is free for other work.  The
                program below is on Canvas, complete; your job is to run it.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-if-not-while">
    <p><term>Check, don't wait.</term> This is deliberately <em>not</em> the Day 5 / Day 7 idiom:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (!(ADC1->ISR & ADC_ISR_EOC)) {}   // Day 7: BLOCKING -- stand here until done

if (TIM14->SR & TIM_SR_UIF) {           // today: glance once, keep moving
    TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;           //   clear the flag
    GPIOA->ODR ^= LED;                  //   toggle the LED
}
// ... and the rest of the loop is free for other work
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The <c>while</c> <term>plants</term> the program at the flag until it rises. The <c>if</c> <term>glances</term> once per trip around the loop and moves on either way.</li>
        <li>That free remainder of the loop is the entire point of a background timer.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#define LED (1U<<5)        // on-board LED
#define PSC_FACTOR 12000   // 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz
#define ARR_FACTOR 500     // 1 kHz / 500 = 2 Hz -> toggle every 500 ms

void tim14_500ms_init();

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Msk;
    GPIOA->MODER |= (GPIO_OUTPUT << GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Pos);
    GPIOA->ODR &= ~(LED);              // LED starts off

    tim14_500ms_init();
    while (1) {
        if (TIM14->SR & TIM_SR_UIF) {  // did a period elapse?
            // Clear UIF in status register
            TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;
            GPIOA->ODR ^= LED;         // toggle LED
        }
        // Could do other stuff here, like poll a button.
    }
    return 1;
}

void tim14_500ms_init() {
    // enable clock access to timer 14 (on APB bus)
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    // Set prescaler value
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;       // starts counting at 0
    // Set auto-reload value
    TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
    // Clear counter
    TIM14->CNT = 0;
    // Enable timer
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
            <activity xml:id="act-timer-polled-run">
                <title>Run the Polled Timer</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Get this program running before anything else today —
                        everything that follows builds on top of it.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-polled-t1">
                    <statement><p>Make a copy of your TemplateProject and call
                    the new project <c>BlinkyTimerPolled</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-polled-t2">
                    <statement><p>Download <c>blinkyTimerPolled.c</c> from
                    Canvas and put it in the new project's <c>Src</c>
                    folder.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-polled-t3">
                    <statement><p>Build and run.  The on-board LED should
                    blink steadily, once per second (500 ms on, 500 ms
                    off).</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <em>Not blinking?</em>  That is expected for some of the room
                on any first build of the day, and there is a two-check rescue
                right here — nobody sits with a dark board alone.  The checks:
                is <c>blinkyTimerPolled.c</c> actually in <c>Src</c> and part
                of the build, and is there exactly <em>one</em> <c>main()</c>
                in the project — a leftover <c>main.c</c> from the template is
                the classic conflict.  If it still will not run, flag it and
                keep moving with the class discussion — a known-good copy of
                the program will be available, and nothing until Part 7 needs
                your own board.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-polled-ladder">
    <p><term>Not blinking?</term> Two checks catch nearly everything:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>1 &#8212; Is <c>blinkyTimerPolled.c</c> in <c>Src</c> and <term>included</term> in the build?</li>
        <li>2 &#8212; Exactly <term>one</term> <c>main()</c> in the project? A leftover template <c>main.c</c> is the classic conflict.</li>
        <li>Still stuck? Flag it, grab the known-good copy, and keep moving &#8212; nothing before Part 7 needs your own board.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Two checks, then the rescue. Nobody debugs a download while the class waits.</caption>
    <note>Checkpoint slide — if the room isn't mostly blinking by minute 32: screen-share the instructor laptop's running build for Part 5, and start the pre-loaded USB stick down the rows. Neither path touches Canvas (the failing channel). Do not debug individual downloads. [Mechanism to confirm with Petra.]</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-rcw0">
            <title>Part 5: Wait — Was That Clear Wrong?</title>
            <p>
                The polled program — yours if it is running, the copy on the
                screen otherwise — contains a line that Day 7 taught you to
                distrust.  To clear the update flag, it says:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;    // today's code: read-modify-write ... ?!
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                On Day 7, clearing a flag in the ADC's status register with
                <c>&amp;= ~</c> was a bug — the fix was a plain assignment,
                <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;</c>, and
                <xref ref="subsec-adc-day7-atomic"/> spent a whole section on
                why the read-modify-write would have wiped out every other
                flag in the register.  And yet the timer code on the screen
                uses <c>&amp;= ~</c> — and its LED is blinking away happily.
                Commit to an answer before reading on: is the line above a
                bug, yes or no?
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-timer-rcw0-lookup">
                <title>Let the Reference Manual Rule</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        The answer is printed in RM0490, and you can find it in
                        under a minute.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-rcw0-t1">
                    <statement><p>Open RM0490 §17.4.3, <em>TIM14 status
                    register (TIM14_SR)</em>.  Under the UIF bit, read the
                    small access-type label printed beneath the bit
                    name.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-rcw0-t2">
                    <statement><p>Now open RM0490 §1.2, <em>List of
                    abbreviations for registers</em>, and find that label's
                    definition.  Compare it with the definition of
                    <c>rc_w1</c>, the ADC flags' type from Day 7.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-sr">
                <caption>The TIM14 status register (RM0490 §17.4.3), with the
                day's answer boxed.  UIF is bit 0, and the access type printed
                under it is <c>rc_w0</c>: read, and clear by writing 0 —
                writing 1 has no effect.  Its neighbors CC1IF and CC1OF are
                <c>rc_w0</c> too.  The ADC's flags were the mirror image,
                <c>rc_w1</c>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/tim14_sr_rcw0.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                UIF is <c>rc_w0</c>: <em>cleared by writing 0; writing 1 has
                no effect.</em>  The ADC's flags were <c>rc_w1</c> — cleared
                by writing 1, writing 0 has no effect.  Same idea, opposite
                polarity.  Now replay Day 7's disaster scenario against an
                <c>rc_w0</c> register.  The read-modify-write reads the whole
                register, clears your one bit in the copy, and writes all of
                it back: a 1 to every flag that read as 1, and a 0 to every
                flag that read as 0.  On an <c>rc_w1</c> register those
                written 1s <em>clear</em> every flag they touch — Day 7's
                disaster.  On an <c>rc_w0</c> register a written 1 does
                nothing, and a written 0 is the clear command — but it only
                ever lands on bits that were already clear.  So no — the line
                is not a bug, and the LED is right to blink.  But notice how
                narrow the escape is: it depends on the access type, a
                property you had to look up, and on nothing else in the
                register having changed between the read and the write-back.
            </p>
            <p>
                That is why the correct habit is the one that works for both
                polarities without re-deriving anything: <em>clear a status
                flag by assigning a mask, never by read-modify-write.</em>
                The mask carries exactly the value that clears your flag and
                leaves every other bit unmolested, whichever the polarity:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;      // rc_w0: 0 to my flag, harmless 1s elsewhere
ADC1->ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;    // rc_w1: 1 to my flag, harmless 0s elsewhere
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The status registers of the STM32C031C6's peripherals split
                between the two conventions — the peripheral designs come from
                different origins, and the clearing method came with them —
                so the access type in the reference manual, not habit, is
                always the authority.  The interrupt version of today's
                program, coming next, clears UIF the correct way.  One more
                reason to prefer the assignment: if the hardware raises
                another flag in this same register between the read and the
                write-back, the read-modify-write writes a 0 over it and the
                event is silently lost — and on Day 9, when your own
                interrupts start landing in the middle of your own main loop,
                that stops being hypothetical.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-waitwhat" room="yes">
    <p><term>The polled code on the screen contains this line:</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;    // clear UIF ... with a read-modify-write?!
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Day 7, same job on the ADC: <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR &amp;= ~ADC_ISR_ADRDY;</c> would have been a <term>bug</term> &#8212; the fix was a plain assignment.</li>
        <li>Yet that LED is blinking away happily right now.</li>
        <li><term>Commit before we look anything up: is this line a bug &#8212; yes or no?</term></li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-sr-rcw0" ref="fig-tim14-sr">
    <p><term>The manual rules: UIF is rc_w0.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>RM0490 &#167;1.2: <c>rc_w0</c> = read; clear by writing 0. <term>Writing 1 has no effect.</term></li>
        <li>Day 7's ADC flags were <c>rc_w1</c> &#8212; the mirror image: clear by writing 1; writing 0 does nothing.</li>
        <li>Replay Day 7's disaster on <em>this</em> register: the read-modify-write writes back a 1 to every flag that read 1 &#8212; harmless here &#8212; and a 0 to every flag that read 0, which on <c>rc_w0</c> <term>is</term> the clear command.</li>
        <li>So: not a bug. It works &#8212; <term>by the grace of the access type</term>, and only while nothing else in SR moves.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Same puzzle as Day 7, opposite polarity — and the answer came off the page.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-moral">
    <p><term>The habit that never needs the replay: assign a mask.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;      // rc_w0: 0 at my flag, harmless 1s everywhere else
ADC1->ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;    // rc_w1: 1 at my flag, harmless 0s everywhere else
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>One shape, correct for <em>both</em> polarities. No read, no modify, nothing to interrupt halfway.</li>
        <li>The STM32C031C6's peripherals split between the two conventions &#8212; the access type in the RM, not habit, is the authority.</li>
        <li>One more strike against read-modify-write: it can be interrupted partway through. Starting Day 9, that stops being hypothetical.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>The ISR you write next clears UIF exactly this way.</caption>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-interrupt-prog">
            <title>Part 6: Programming an Interrupt</title>
            <p>
                Polling the flag freed the loop from blocking, but the loop
                still spends every iteration asking.  The pre-class video's
                answer is the interrupt: let the update event <em>call a
                function</em> — push the machine state, vector to your
                handler, pop, resume
                (<xref ref="fig-interrupt-execution"/>).  Two reminders from
                the video before the mechanics, because both collide with
                habits.  First, the ISR we are about to write is an Interrupt
                <em>Service Routine</em> — a function — not an interrupt and
                status <em>register</em> like <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR</c>; same
                letters, different thing.  Second, if you have used Arduino:
                there is no <c>attachInterrupt()</c> here.  Arduino let you
                hand any function to a registry at run time; on this bare
                machine, you are about to do yourself the three things that
                call used to hide — turn on the source, allow it through the
                controller, and supply the handler under the name the
                hardware's table expects.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Enable the interrupt in the timer.</term>  The timer
                must be told to <em>raise</em> an interrupt at each update
                event, not just set the flag.  That switch is the UIE bit
                (update interrupt enable) in <c>TIM14_DIER</c>:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
TIM14->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;   // update event now raises an interrupt request
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Two bit names one letter apart are about to share the stage,
                so say it now: UIE (in DIER) <em>enables</em> the request;
                UIF (in SR) <em>reports</em> that the event happened.  Do not
                swap them.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-regmap-interrupt">
                <caption>The register map again, with today's addition: UIE in
                <c>TIM14_DIER</c> (interrupt enable register) makes each
                update event raise an interrupt request.  CEN and UIF, from
                the polled version, are dimmed — they keep their jobs.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/tim14_regmap_interrupt.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <term>Enable the interrupt in the NVIC.</term>  The request
                now reaches the NVIC — which, remember, ignores every source
                until told otherwise.  CMSIS provides the function, and the
                device header provides a named constant for each interrupt
                line:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn);    // allow TIM14's requests through the NVIC
]]></code></program>
            <activity xml:id="act-timer-vector-lookup">
                <title>Where the Names Come From</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        <c>TIM14_IRQn</c> and the handler name you are about
                        to need are not folklore — both trace to one table.
                    </p>
                    </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-vector-t1">
                    <statement><p>Before looking: the vector table lists every
                    interrupt source on the chip, numbered from 0.  Write
                    down a guess — roughly where in the list does TIM14
                    sit?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-vector-t2">
                    <statement><p>Now open RM0490 §11.3, Table 40, <em>Vector
                    table</em>, and find the TIM14 row.  Note its position
                    number and its acronym.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-vector-table">
                <caption>An excerpt of RM0490's Table 40, the vector table:
                positions 14 through 23, with the header row and the TIM14 row
                boxed.  TIM14's interrupt is position 19, and its vector — the
                address the CPU fetches the handler's location from — is
                0x0000_008C.  TIM3, TIM16, TIM17, and I2C1 occupy nearby
                positions.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/vector_table_tim14.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                TIM14 is position 19, and one interrupt channel goes by three
                names, each mechanical once you see the pattern.  In Table 40
                it is the row at position 19.  In C code it is
                <c>TIM14_IRQn</c> — the device header defines one such
                constant per populated row, mirroring the position column,
                and it is what <c>NVIC_EnableIRQ()</c> takes.
            </p>
            <p>
                The third name is the one you must get exactly right.  The
                handler function is <c>TIM14_IRQHandler</c>, and that name is
                not yours to invent — it is listed in the project's
                <term>startup file</term>, <c>startup_stm32c031ctx.s</c>: an
                assembly file, generated with your project, that runs before
                <c>main()</c> and wires each official handler name into its
                vector-table slot.  It sits in your project tree alongside
                <c>Src</c>.  You will read that file, never edit it: find the
                name, select it, copy it.  A name that is even one character
                off still compiles and still links — the linker simply never
                places your function in the table, and the slot keeps the
                default handler the startup file installed there.  Your ISR
                never runs.  Depending on what that default handler does, the
                board may also stop responding entirely — no LED, no serial
                output — so a dead board and a dark LED are the <em>same</em>
                diagnosis, and rung 3 of Part 7's ladder is where you check
                it.
            </p>
            <!-- TODO (Petra): fig-startup-file — annotated Project Explorer
                 screenshot (startup file's folder circled, house style of
                 fig-exclude-from-build) + a crop of the file showing the
                 TIM14_IRQHandler line.  Referenced from the handler-name
                 slide once supplied.  Also confirm the exact filename:
                 CubeIDE normally emits startup_stm32c031c6tx.s. -->
            <p>
                <term>Give the ISR a way to talk to main.</term>  The
                video's flag pattern (written version:
                <xref ref="subsec-timers-ref-isr"/>) is
                exactly what today's ISR needs, so bring it back to mind: a
                global flag, declared <c>volatile</c> because the compiler
                cannot see the ISR's write from inside <c>main()</c>'s loop;
                the ISR sets it; the loop checks it, clears it, and does the
                work.  Without <c>volatile</c> the optimizer may read the
                flag once and never again — the blink simply stops happening,
                with no error anywhere.
            </p>
            <p>
                One last piece of housekeeping, visible in the code you are
                about to complete: the initialization is bracketed by
                <c>__disable_irq()</c> and <c>__enable_irq()</c>, which turn
                all interrupts off globally during setup so nothing fires
                while half-configured, and the ISR itself does the same around
                its body.  Inside the ISR the bracket is belt-and-suspenders —
                the Cortex-M0+ already holds same- or lower-priority
                interrupts pending while a handler runs — and it is harmless
                here because TIM14 is the only active interrupt source, with
                every source at the same default priority.  Once a system
                mixes priorities, disabling interrupts globally inside a
                handler is exactly what you do not want: it makes every other
                interrupt wait out this one.  Use it today; do not carry it
                forward as a default habit.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-dier" ref="fig-tim14-regmap-interrupt">
    <p><term>Step 1 of 3: the timer must raise the interrupt.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><c>TIM14-&gt;DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;</c> &#8212; each update event now raises an interrupt request, not just the flag.</li>
        <li>DIER is the peripheral-side switch: the timer asking to be heard.</li>
        <li>One letter apart, two different jobs: <term>UIE</term> (DIER) <em>enables</em> the request; <term>UIF</term> (SR) <em>reports</em> the event. Don't swap them.</li>
        <li>CEN and UIF keep their jobs from the polled version &#8212; nothing else in the init changes.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-nvic">
    <p><term>Step 2 of 3: the NVIC must let it through.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn);   // CMSIS function, device-header constant
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The NVIC ignores every source until told otherwise &#8212; one enable switch per interrupt line, all off at reset.</li>
        <li>Two switches, two sides: UIE is the timer raising its hand; the NVIC enable is the controller agreeing to look.</li>
        <li>Forget either one and you get silence. (That is rung 4 of today's ladder.)</li>
        <li>Where does <c>TIM14_IRQn</c> come from? One table behind all the names &#8212; next.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-vector" ref="fig-vector-table">
    <p><term>The table behind the names.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>The row you predicted: <term>position 19</term>, acronym TIM14, vector address 0x0000_008C &#8212; RM0490 &#167;11.3, Table 40.</li>
        <li>The C constant you just used: <c>TIM14_IRQn</c> &#8212; the device header defines one per populated row, mirroring the position column. Handed to you, not looked up.</li>
        <li>One more name comes from this table &#8212; the handler's &#8212; and it gets its own slide, because it is the one you must get exactly right.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-handler-name">
    <p><term>The third name: the handler — copy it, don't type it.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>The function you are about to write must be named <term>exactly</term> <c>TIM14_IRQHandler</c>.</li>
        <li>Where it comes from: the <term>startup file</term>, <c>startup_stm32c031ctx.s</c> &#8212; an assembly file generated with your project (it sits in the project tree alongside <c>Src</c>) that runs before <c>main()</c> and wires each official handler name into its vector-table slot.</li>
        <li>You <term>read</term> that file, never edit it: find the name, select it, <term>copy</term> it.</li>
        <li>A name one character off still compiles <em>and links</em> &#8212; your function never enters the table, and the slot keeps the startup file's default handler. Symptom: no blink, possibly no output at all.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>fig-startup-file (Project Explorer screenshot) pending from Petra — add ref="fig-startup-file" here once it exists.</note>
</slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-code">
            <title>Part 7: Writing blinkyTimerInt.c</title>
            <p>
                Now assemble <c>blinkyTimerInt.c</c>.  The skeleton below is
                the polled program from <xref ref="subsec-day8-polled"/>
                with its heart moved: the five init lines you already ran are
                given, and the four <c>// TODO</c> clusters are the interrupt
                machinery from Part 6.  Note what is <em>gone</em>: the main
                loop no longer touches the timer at all.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/*
 * blinkyTimerInt.c -- blink the on-board LED from a TIM14 update interrupt
 *
 * Four blanks to fill in.  Everything you need was in Parts 5 and 6:
 *
 *   TODO 1   raise an interrupt per update      TIM14->DIER
 *   TODO 2   let it through the controller      NVIC_EnableIRQ( ... )
 *   TODO 3   the handler itself                 exact name from the startup file
 *   TODO 4   the shared flag                    one keyword matters
 */
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#define LED (1U<<5)        // on-board LED
#define PSC_FACTOR 12000   // 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz
#define ARR_FACTOR 500     // 1 kHz / 500 = 2 Hz

void tim14_500ms_interrupt_init();

// TODO 4a -- declare the flag the ISR will share with main.
//            Which keyword must the declaration carry, and why?


int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Msk;
    GPIOA->MODER |= (GPIO_OUTPUT << GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Pos);
    GPIOA->ODR &= ~(LED);          // LED starts off

    // TODO 4b -- initialize the flag ...

    tim14_500ms_interrupt_init();

    while (1) {
        // TODO 4c -- when the flag is set: clear it, toggle the LED.
        //            The timer registers appear NOWHERE in this loop.

    }
}

void tim14_500ms_interrupt_init() {
    __disable_irq();               // given: no interrupts during setup
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->CNT = 0;

    // TODO 1 -- enable the update interrupt in the timer itself


    // TODO 2 -- enable TIM14's line in the NVIC


    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
    __enable_irq();                // given: setup done, interrupts on
}

// TODO 3 -- write the ISR.  Its name must be EXACTLY the handler name from
//           the vector table lookup -- copy it from the startup file, don't
//           type it from memory.  No arguments, no return value.  Inside,
//           bracketed by __disable_irq(); ... __enable_irq(); do only two
//           things: clear UIF the Part 5 way (TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;),
//           and set the flag.
]]></code></program>
            <activity xml:id="act-timer-interrupt">
                <title>Make the Timer Do the Calling</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        The skeleton is on Canvas as
                        <c>blinkyTimerInt.c</c>.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-int-t1">
                    <statement><p>Add <c>blinkyTimerInt.c</c> to your
                    project's <c>Src</c> folder.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-int-t1b">
                    <statement><p>Swap the build: right-click
                    <c>blinkyTimerPolled.c</c> and choose <em>Resource
                    Configurations → Exclude from Build</em>, click <em>Select
                    All</em>, then OK — and make sure <c>blinkyTimerInt.c</c>
                    is <em>not</em> excluded
                    (<xref ref="fig-exclude-from-build"/>).  Two
                    <c>main()</c>s cannot coexist in one build.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-int-t2">
                    <statement><p>Fill TODO 1: the update-interrupt enable in
                    the timer.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-int-t3">
                    <statement><p>Fill TODO 2: the NVIC enable.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-int-t4">
                    <statement><p>Fill TODO 3: write the ISR, name copied from
                    the startup file.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-int-t5">
                    <statement><p>Fill TODO 4: the <c>volatile</c> flag —
                    declared, initialized, and consumed in the loop.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-int-t6">
                    <statement><p>Build, run, and watch the LED blink at the
                    same steady rate as before — now with an empty-handed main
                    loop.  Done early?  Make it toggle every 250 ms without
                    touching PSC; then go back to 500 ms and make it toggle
                    every 250 ms without touching ARR.  Now check both against
                    a 48 MHz clock: <em>neither</em> survives — why not, and
                    what single number would you change to fix either
                    one?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <em>LED not blinking?</em>  First, one triage check: is the
                board <em>completely</em> dead — no LED, no serial output at
                all?  Jump straight to rung 3.  Otherwise walk the ladder in
                order — it isolates the fault:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Does the project build cleanly, with
                    <c>blinkyTimerInt.c</c> included and
                    <c>blinkyTimerPolled.c</c> excluded via <em>Resource
                    Configurations → Exclude from Build</em>?</li>
                <li>Swap back for a moment: does the <em>polled</em> version
                    still blink?  If yes, your timer configuration is fine —
                    the problem is in the interrupt path, which narrows it to
                    the next two rungs.</li>
                <li>Is the ISR's name <em>exactly</em>
                    <c>TIM14_IRQHandler</c> — copied from the startup file,
                    not typed?  A near-miss name builds and links without a
                    single warning; your function is never in the table, so it
                    never runs — and the board may look completely dead rather
                    than merely dark.</li>
                <li>Did you enable <em>both</em> halves — UIE in
                    <c>TIM14_DIER</c> <em>and</em> the NVIC line?  Either one
                    alone produces silence.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Out of time before the four blanks are filled?  That is a
                clock problem, not a bug.  The complete file goes up on the
                screen at the end of the part, and on Canvas after class —
                copy it and move on to <xref ref="subsec-day8-homework"/>
                with a working reference.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-skeleton">
    <p><term>blinkyTimerInt.c — the polled program with its heart moved.</term> The five init lines are the ones already running on your board; four blanks are new.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// TODO 1   raise an interrupt per update      TIM14->DIER
// TODO 2   let it through the controller      NVIC_EnableIRQ( ... )
// TODO 3   the handler itself                 exact name, copied from startup file
// TODO 4   the shared flag                    declare / initialize / consume; one keyword matters

void tim14_500ms_interrupt_init() {
    __disable_irq();               // given: no interrupts during setup
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->CNT = 0;

    // TODO 1 -- enable the update interrupt in the timer itself

    // TODO 2 -- enable TIM14's line in the NVIC

    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
    __enable_irq();                // given: setup done, interrupts on
}

// TODO 3 -- the ISR: exact name; no arguments, no return value.
//           Inside (bracketed by __disable_irq()/__enable_irq()), do TWO things:
//           clear UIF the Part 5 way (TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;), set the flag.

// TODO 4 -- in main: the timer registers appear NOWHERE in the loop.
//           When the flag is set: clear it, toggle the LED.
]]></code></program>
    <caption>Everything new was on the last four slides. The full file is on Canvas.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-ladder">
    <p><term>LED not blinking? Walk the ladder in order.</term> Board completely dead &#8212; no LED, no serial output at all? Jump to rung 3.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>1 &#8212; Build clean? <c>blinkyTimerInt.c</c> <term>included</term>, <c>blinkyTimerPolled.c</c> <term>excluded</term>, via <em>Resource Configurations &#8594; Exclude from Build</em>?</li>
        <li>2 &#8212; Swap back for a moment: does the <term>polled</term> version still blink? If yes, your timer config is fine &#8212; the fault is in the interrupt path (rungs 3 and 4).</li>
        <li>3 &#8212; ISR name <term>exactly</term> <c>TIM14_IRQHandler</c>, copied from the startup file? A near-miss builds and links with zero warnings &#8212; and the board may look completely dead rather than merely dark.</li>
        <li>4 &#8212; <term>Both</term> enables present? UIE in <c>TIM14-&gt;DIER</c> <em>and</em> <c>NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn)</c>. Either alone is silence.</li>
        <li>Done early instead? 250 ms toggle <em>without touching PSC</em>; then <em>without touching ARR</em>. Check both against a 48 MHz clock: <term>neither survives</term> &#8212; why not?</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Four rungs, in order — each one rules out a layer.</caption>
    <note>Stays up for all of Part 7. Out of time is a clock problem, not a bug: the solution slide is next, and the file goes to Canvas after class.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-stretch-solution">
    <p><term>Solution — the 250 ms stretch, and why neither version survives 48 MHz.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Without touching PSC:  ARR_FACTOR 500 -> 250.  Tick stays 1 ms; 250 ticks = 250 ms.
// Without touching ARR:  PSC_FACTOR 12000 -> 6000.  Tick becomes 0.5 ms; 500 ticks = 250 ms.

// At 48 MHz, BOTH give:  t = (PSC+1)(ARR+1) / f  =  3,000,000 / 48,000,000  =  62.5 ms.
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The period depends only on the <term>product</term> (PSC+1)&#215;(ARR+1) and on f &#8212; never on how the product splits between the two registers. That is the point of the exercise.</li>
        <li>The fix is to grow the product by 4: for example PSC_FACTOR 12000 &#8594; 48000 with ARR_FACTOR 250 gives 12,000,000 cycles = 250 ms at 48 MHz &#8212; and 47999 still fits in 16 bits.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-solution">
    <p><term>Solution — blinkyTimerInt.c</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#define LED (1U<<5)        // blinking the on-board LED
#define PSC_FACTOR 12000   // 12 MHz/12000 = 1 kHz
#define ARR_FACTOR 500     // 1 kHz/500 = 2 Hz

void tim14_500ms_interrupt_init();
volatile int timerElapsed;             // TODO 4a: the shared flag, volatile

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Msk;
    GPIOA->MODER |= (GPIO_OUTPUT << GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Pos);
    GPIOA->ODR &= ~(LED);              // Start LED as off
    timerElapsed = 0;                  // TODO 4b: initialize flag
    tim14_500ms_interrupt_init();

    while (1) {
        if (timerElapsed) {            // TODO 4c: consume the flag
            timerElapsed = 0;          //   clear flag
            GPIOA->ODR ^= LED;         //   toggle LED
        }
    }
}

void tim14_500ms_interrupt_init() {
    __disable_irq();
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->CNT = 0;
    TIM14->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;       // TODO 1: enable update interrupt
    NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn);        // TODO 2: enable in NVIC
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
    __enable_irq();
}

void TIM14_IRQHandler(void) {          // TODO 3: exact vector-table name
    __disable_irq();
    TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;           //   clear UIF -- the Part 5 idiom
    timerElapsed = 1;                  //   set global flag
    __enable_irq();
}
]]></code></program>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day8-homework">
            <title>Part 8: Before Next Class</title>
            <p>
                Today's pattern — configure a timer, let its interrupt do the
                timekeeping, keep the main loop for real work — is precisely
                what Lab 5 will ask of you at larger scale, where a timer
                interrupt paces the ADC in a temperature controller.  The
                homework is the bridge.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-timer-homework">
                <title>Homework: ADCPot with a Blinking Heartbeat</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Due at the start of Tuesday's class, submitted per the
                        Canvas assignment.
                    </p>
                    </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-hw-t1">
                    <statement><p>Modify your <c>ADCPot.c</c> from Day 7 so
                    that, in addition to performing the ADC readings, the
                    on-board LED blinks at a steady rate of <em>one-third of a
                    second on, one-third of a second off</em>, using a timer
                    interrupt.  Hint: you may wish to toggle the LED directly
                    in the ISR — consider what the main loop is busy doing,
                    and for how long at a stretch.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-timer-hw-t2">
                    <statement><p>Choose your prescaler and auto-reload values
                    so the period is <em>exact</em>, and show the arithmetic
                    in a comment.  One-third of a second does not divide as
                    conveniently as 500 ms did — more than one pair works, but
                    not every pair you might like.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-blinky-flow">
                <caption>The homework's structure, from the class deck: the
                main loop cycles through start-conversion, read, print, and
                its delay, while the timer interrupt arrives on its own
                schedule; each arrival runs the ISR — clear the flag, toggle
                the LED — and returns exactly where main was suspended.  The
                two rhythms never disturb each other.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/adc_blinky_flow.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                And a challenge, for fun — nothing to submit: make the blink
                <em>asymmetric</em>, 200 ms on and 800 ms off.  One timer, two
                different intervals: the code must reprogram ARR on the fly
                and remember which phase it is in.  A state machine with two
                states does it in a dozen lines.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-hw-flow" ref="fig-adc-blinky-flow">
    <caption>Two rhythms, one CPU: main cycles read-print-delay; the ISR toggles the LED whenever the timer says so.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-hw-solution">
    <p><term>Solution — ADCPot with a timer-interrupt heartbeat.</term>
    The divisor search first: 1/3 s at 12 MHz is 4,000,000 cycles = prescale factor &#215; count.
    The comfortable 12,000 &#215; 333.3&#8230; fails (fraction); 4000 &#215; 1000 is exact and both fit 16 bits.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"

#define LED        (1U<<5)   // on-board LED, PA5
#define PSC_FACTOR 4000      // 12 MHz / 4000 = 3 kHz
#define ARR_FACTOR 1000      // 3 kHz / 1000 -> toggle every 1/3 s exactly

// Unchanged from Day 7:
void     pa0_adc_init(void);
void     start_conversion(void);
uint16_t adc_read(void);

void tim14_third_s_interrupt_init(void);

int main(void) {
    uint16_t sensor_value;
    uart2_init();
    printf("\n\rADCPot with heartbeat\r\n");

    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;         // LED pin
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Msk;
    GPIOA->MODER |= (GPIO_OUTPUT << GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Pos);
    GPIOA->ODR &= ~(LED);

    pa0_adc_init();
    tim14_third_s_interrupt_init();

    while (1) {                                 // main keeps its old rhythm
        start_conversion();
        sensor_value = adc_read();
        printf("Sensor value: %d\r\n", sensor_value);
        delay_ms(1000);
    }
}

void tim14_third_s_interrupt_init(void) {
    __disable_irq();
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->ARR = ARR_FACTOR - 1;
    TIM14->CNT = 0;
    TIM14->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;
    NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn);
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
    __enable_irq();
}

// Per the hint: toggle directly in the ISR.  Main spends most of its life
// inside delay_ms(1000) -- a flag it only checks between laps would blink
// raggedly.  The interrupt lands even mid-delay; the blink keeps its rhythm.
void TIM14_IRQHandler(void) {
    __disable_irq();
    TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;   // clear UIF
    GPIOA->ODR ^= LED;         // toggle LED directly in the ISR
    __enable_irq();
}
]]></code></program>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-challenge-solution">
    <p><term>Solution — T_on &#8800; T_off.</term> <c>tim14_ms_interrupt_init(ms)</c> is Day 8's init with the period as a parameter.
    The mid-flight ARR write works because ARPE (auto-reload preload) is 0 at reset, so a new ARR takes effect immediately &#8212;
    with ARPE set it would be buffered until the next update event and the phases would lag by one.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"

#define LED        (1U<<5)   // on-board LED
#define PSC_FACTOR 12000     // 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz: one tick per ms
#define T_ON       200       // ms
#define T_OFF      800       // ms

typedef enum {LEDON, LEDOFF} state_t;
volatile state_t state;
volatile int timerFlag;

void tim14_ms_interrupt_init(uint16_t period_ms);

void TIM14_IRQHandler(void) {
    __disable_irq();
    TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;   // clear UIF
    timerFlag = 1;
    __enable_irq();
}

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Msk;
    GPIOA->MODER |= (GPIO_OUTPUT << GPIO_MODER_MODE5_Pos);
    GPIOA->ODR &= ~(LED);        // LED is off
    state = LEDOFF;
    timerFlag = 0;
    tim14_ms_interrupt_init(T_OFF);
    while (1) {
        if (timerFlag) {
            GPIOA->ODR ^= LED;
            switch (state) {
                case LEDOFF:                  // just turned ON
                    TIM14->ARR = T_ON - 1;    // this phase lasts T_ON
                    state = LEDON;
                    break;
                case LEDON:                   // just turned OFF
                    TIM14->ARR = T_OFF - 1;   // this phase lasts T_OFF
                    state = LEDOFF;
                    break;
            }
            timerFlag = 0;
        }
    }
}

// Day 8's init with the period as a parameter -- the only change is ARR.
void tim14_ms_interrupt_init(uint16_t period_ms) {
    __disable_irq();
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    TIM14->PSC = PSC_FACTOR - 1;   // one tick per ms
    TIM14->ARR = period_ms - 1;    // the period is the argument
    TIM14->CNT = 0;
    TIM14->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;
    NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn);
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
    __enable_irq();
}
]]></code></program>
</slide>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- ROUGH: Day 9 (EXTI) material below this line has NOT yet been    -->
    <!-- authored through the chapter process.  Assume nothing in it is   -->
    <!-- correct until checked against Step 0 ground truth for Day 9.     -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-gpio-interrupts">
        <title>External (GPIO) Interrupts</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A button press can also trigger an interrupt, letting the program respond
                immediately rather than polling.  The STM32 routes GPIO pins through an
                <term>EXTI</term> (External Interrupt) multiplexer before reaching the NVIC.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-exti-mux">
            <title>The EXTI Multiplexer</title>
            <p>
                Each GPIO port has pins numbered 0–15.  The EXTI block has 16 lines (EXTI0–15).
                Pin <m>n</m> of any port maps to line EXTIn — but only one port can be
                connected to each EXTI line at a time.  The <c>EXTI_EXTICRx</c> registers
                select which port drives each line.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-mux">
                <caption>EXTI GPIO multiplexer.  Each EXTI line (0–15) can be connected
                to pin <m>n</m> of Port A, B, C, D, or F via the EXTICR registers.
                Only one port per line at a time.  EXTI4 would be connected to PB4 by
                writing the Port B code into EXTI_EXTICR2 bits [7:0].</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide20_f495384e.png" width="48%"/>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide20_1407f444.png" width="48%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-exti-config">
            <title>Configuring a GPIO Interrupt on PB4</title>
            <p>
                Setting up an external interrupt for the button on PB4 (falling edge,
                active-low with pull-up) requires five steps.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-button">
                <caption>Button on PB4 with pull-up and debounce capacitor.  The falling
                edge occurs when the button is pressed (pin goes from HIGH to LOW).
                We configure EXTI to trigger on the falling edge.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide21_b404af41.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-ftsr">
                <caption>EXTI Falling-Trigger Selection Register (FTSR1).  Setting bit 4
                enables falling-edge detection on EXTI4.  A separate rising-trigger register
                (RTSR1) handles rising edges; both can be set simultaneously for any-edge
                detection.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide29_1668932b.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-imr">
                <caption>EXTI Interrupt Mask Register (IMR1).  Setting bit 4 unmasks EXTI4,
                allowing it to generate an interrupt request to the NVIC.  A masked line
                is ignored even if an edge occurs.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide31_873b01af.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-nvic">
                <caption>NVIC enable for EXTI lines 4–15.  On the STM32C031, EXTI lines
                4–15 share a single NVIC interrupt line, <c>EXTI4_15_IRQn</c>.  The ISR
                must check the EXTI_FPR1 register to determine which line triggered.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide33_8d3f7a34.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void pb4_exti_init(void) {
    __disable_irq();

    // 1. Configure PB4 as input with pull-up
    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOBEN;
    GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(3U << 8);
    GPIOB->PUPDR |=  (1U << 8);   // pull-up

    // 2. Connect EXTI4 to Port B
    EXTI->EXTICR[1] &= ~EXTI_EXTICR2_EXTI4_Msk;
    EXTI->EXTICR[1] |=  (EXTI_EXTICR2_EXTI4_PB);

    // 3. Enable falling-edge detection on EXTI4
    EXTI->FTSR1 |= EXTI_FTSR1_FT4;

    // 4. Unmask EXTI4
    EXTI->IMR1 |= EXTI_IMR1_IM4;

    // 5. Enable NVIC for EXTI4_15
    NVIC_EnableIRQ(EXTI4_15_IRQn);

    __enable_irq();
}

volatile uint8_t buttonPushed = 0;

void EXTI4_15_IRQHandler(void) {
    __disable_irq();
    if (EXTI->FPR1 & EXTI_FPR1_FPIF4) {
        EXTI->FPR1 = EXTI_FPR1_FPIF4;  // clear pending flag (write-1-to-clear)
        buttonPushed = 1;
    }
    __enable_irq();
}
]]></code></program>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-exticr">
                <caption>EXTI_EXTICR2 register showing Port B selected for EXTI4.  The
                four-bit field [7:0] holds a port code: 0x01 = Port B.  Bits [15:8],
                [23:16], [31:24] configure EXTI5, EXTI6, EXTI7 respectively.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide24_3eaa0750.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-isr">
                <caption>EXTI ISR checking and clearing the pending flag.  The Falling
                Pending Register (FPR1) has one bit per EXTI line; it is set by hardware
                on a falling edge and cleared by writing 1 to it.  The ISR must clear
                FPR1 before returning or the interrupt will re-fire immediately.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide38_8b444d34.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exti-int-flow">
                <caption>The complete interrupt flow for a GPIO falling edge.  After the
                EXTI detects the edge it sets the pending bit and signals the NVIC.  The
                NVIC saves CPU state and vectors to the ISR.  After the ISR returns, the
                CPU restores state and resumes the main loop exactly where it left off.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09-Interrupts(2)/slide35_a320de49.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-interrupt-pitfalls">
            <title>Common Interrupt Pitfalls</title>
            <p>
                Modifying shared data inside an ISR can cause subtle bugs.  If the ISR
                increments a multi-byte counter while main is reading it, the read may
                see a partially-updated value.  The safest pattern: use the ISR only to
                set a single-byte <c>volatile</c> flag; let main do all the work when
                it sees that flag set.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-timers-reference">
        <title>Reference: The TIM14 Hardware</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Lookup material for the registers and conventions this chapter
                uses.  Nothing here is new — it is the same information,
                arranged for the moment you come back needing one fact.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timers-ref-registers">
            <title>Register Summary</title>
            <table xml:id="table-tim14-registers">
                <title>The TIM14 registers used in this chapter</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Register</cell>
                        <cell>Offset</cell>
                        <cell>Bit(s) used</cell>
                        <cell>Purpose</cell>
                        <cell>RM0490</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_CR1</c></cell>
                        <cell>0x00</cell>
                        <cell>CEN (bit 0)</cell>
                        <cell>Counter enable — set to start counting</cell>
                        <cell>§17.4.1</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_DIER</c></cell>
                        <cell>0x0C</cell>
                        <cell>UIE (bit 0)</cell>
                        <cell>Update interrupt enable — update events raise an interrupt request</cell>
                        <cell>§17.4.2</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_SR</c></cell>
                        <cell>0x10</cell>
                        <cell>UIF (bit 0)</cell>
                        <cell>Update interrupt flag — set by hardware at each update event; <c>rc_w0</c>, cleared by software</cell>
                        <cell>§17.4.3</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_CNT</c></cell>
                        <cell>0x24</cell>
                        <cell>CNT[15:0]</cell>
                        <cell>The running count — zero it before starting</cell>
                        <cell>§17.4.8</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_PSC</c></cell>
                        <cell>0x28</cell>
                        <cell>PSC[15:0]</cell>
                        <cell>Prescaler — counter clock is input clock ÷ (PSC + 1)</cell>
                        <cell>§17.4.9</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>TIM14_ARR</c></cell>
                        <cell>0x2C</cell>
                        <cell>ARR[15:0]</cell>
                        <cell>Auto-reload — counter wraps to 0 after reaching this value</cell>
                        <cell>§17.4.10</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                The period formula, in one place: with input clock
                <m>f</m> (12 MHz in this course),
                <md>t_{period} = \frac{(\text{PSC}+1)\times(\text{ARR}+1)}{f}</md>
                where PSC and ARR are the values <em>as written</em> to the
                registers.  Both are 16 bits; neither written value can exceed
                65,535.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timers-ref-access-types">
            <title>Status-Register Access Types</title>
            <p>
                RM0490 §1.2, <em>List of abbreviations for registers</em>,
                defines the access-type labels printed under every bit of
                every register description.  The ones this course meets:
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-access-types">
                <title>Register access types (RM0490 §1.2)</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Label</cell>
                        <cell>Meaning</cell>
                        <cell>How software clears it</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>rw</c></cell>
                        <cell>Read/write</cell>
                        <cell>Write any value</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>r</c></cell>
                        <cell>Read-only</cell>
                        <cell>Software cannot — hardware manages it</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>rc_w0</c></cell>
                        <cell>Read; clear by writing 0 (writing 1 has no effect)</cell>
                        <cell>Assign a mask with 0 at the flag: <c>REG = ~FLAG;</c></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>rc_w1</c></cell>
                        <cell>Read; clear by writing 1 (writing 0 has no effect)</cell>
                        <cell>Assign a mask with 1 at the flag: <c>REG = FLAG;</c></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>rs</c></cell>
                        <cell>Read; software can set by writing 1; writing 0 has no effect</cell>
                        <cell>Not by writing — §1.2 defines only the write-0-no-effect behavior (ADEN, ADSTART)</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                The habit worth keeping: clear status flags by assigning the
                mask, never by read-modify-write.  The assignment is correct
                for both polarities.  <c>&amp;= ~</c> is destructive on
                <c>rc_w1</c> bits, and on <c>rc_w0</c> bits it is safe only
                as long as no other flag in the register is raised between
                the read and the write-back — and the label in the reference
                manual is the only way to know which polarity you have.
            </p>
            <p>
                One honest footnote on <c>REG = ~FLAG</c>: the mask also
                writes 1s into the register's reserved bits, which RM0490
                §1.2 says to keep at their reset value.  It is safe on this
                family's timer status registers — it is what ST's own
                low-level driver does — but reserved-bit behavior is worth
                checking per register rather than assumed.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timers-ref-block">
            <title>The Full TIM14 Block Diagram</title>
            <p>
                The reading reduces TIM14 to rate, range, wrap
                (<xref ref="subsec-timer-hardware"/>).  The chip's own
                picture of the peripheral shows how much more is bolted
                around that core — input filtering, capture/compare, output
                control — all of it dimmed here and all of it returning in
                the motor chapters.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tim14-block">
                <caption>TIM14's full block diagram (RM0490 Figure 165) with
                the timing core highlighted: the clock path into the
                prescaler (CK_INT through the trigger controller to CK_PSC,
                blue); the <em>prescaled</em> counter clock CK_CNT coming out
                of the prescaler (red); the 16-bit counter CNT (green); and
                the auto-reload register that sets the counter's upper limit
                (orange).  The dimmed capture/compare machinery is real and
                returns in the motor chapters — for periodic timing only the
                highlighted path matters.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/tim14_block_annotated.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8v-timer" ref="fig-tim14-block">
    <ul>
        <li>A hardware timer is a circuit that counts clock pulses &#8212; separate from the CPU, running whether the CPU is busy or not.</li>
        <li>Three parts do today's work: the <clr c="blue">clock path</clr> in, the <clr c="red">prescaler's output</clr> &#8212; the divided-down counting rate &#8212; and the <clr c="green">counter</clr> itself.</li>
        <li>The <clr c="orange">auto-reload register</clr> sets the upper limit: count up to it, wrap to 0, and announce an <term>update event</term>.</li>
        <li>Rate &#215; count = period. Everything else in the picture is dimmed because it belongs to later chapters.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Video slide — the timer core on the chip's own diagram.</caption>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timers-ref-mechanism">
            <title>The Interrupt Mechanism, in Full</title>
            <p>
                The pre-class video walks this machinery in motion; this is
                the written version.  Start with what the CPU does normally
                (<xref ref="fig-normal-execution"/>): the program counter
                (PC) points at the next instruction in flash; the instruction
                is fetched, decoded, and executed; the PC advances; repeat.
                You met this machinery — the register file, SP, LR, and PC —
                in <xref ref="subsec-processor-arch"/>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-normal-execution">
                <caption>Normal program execution.  The program counter steps
                through instructions in flash memory; each is fetched into the
                instruction register, decoded, and executed by the
                datapath.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/slide30_458cd4b0.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                A function call interrupts that straight line deliberately
                (<xref ref="fig-function-call"/>).  Compiler-generated code at
                the top of the function saves whichever registers that
                function will disturb, the PC is loaded with the function's
                start address, and the function runs.  On return, the saved
                registers are restored and execution continues at the
                instruction after the call.  What gets saved is the
                <em>compiler's</em> decision, made when the code was built —
                and the stack is what makes the round trip safe: everything
                needed to pick up where the program left off was saved before
                leaving.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-function-call">
                <caption>What a function call does.  Call: compiler-generated
                code at the function's entry saves the registers this function
                will disturb onto the stack, and the function's address goes
                into the PC.  Return: the saved registers are restored and
                execution resumes at the next instruction.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/slide31_ff36fa00.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8v-callstack" ref="fig-function-call">
    <ul>
        <li>Normal execution: the program counter walks instruction to instruction through flash.</li>
        <li>A function call is a planned detour: save what you'll disturb onto the <term>stack</term>, jump, run, restore, resume at the next instruction.</li>
        <li>The stack is what makes the round trip safe &#8212; everything needed to pick up where you left off was saved before leaving.</li>
        <li>Hold that thought: an interrupt is this exact trick, played by hardware.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Video slide — the function call, the trick the interrupt borrows.</caption>
</slide>
            <p>
                An <term>interrupt</term> is a function call that hardware
                makes (<xref ref="fig-interrupt-execution"/>).  When a
                peripheral raises its interrupt signal, the CPU finishes the
                instruction it is on, pushes the machine state onto the stack
                exactly as a call would, and then — this is the only new part —
                looks up where to jump in a <term>vector table</term>: a table
                in flash, at a fixed location, holding the starting address of
                one handler function per interrupt source.  The CPU loads that
                address into the PC and runs the function, called an
                <term>Interrupt Service Routine</term>.  When it returns, the
                saved state is popped and the interrupted program resumes,
                unaware anything happened.  An interrupt can arrive at
                <em>any</em> instruction — even in the middle of
                <c>delay_ms()</c> — and the stack machinery makes the detour
                invisible to the interrupted code.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-mechanism" ref="fig-interrupt-execution">
    <ul>
        <li>An interrupt is a function call <term>hardware</term> makes: finish the current instruction, push the machine state, vector to the handler, pop, resume.</li>
        <li>It can land between <em>any</em> two instructions &#8212; even inside <c>delay_ms()</c> &#8212; and the interrupted code never knows.</li>
        <li>Vocabulary guard: this ISR is a Service <term>Routine</term> &#8212; a function you write &#8212; not the status <term>register</term> ISR of <c>USART2-&gt;ISR</c> / <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR</c>. Same letters, different thing.</li>
        <li>Arduino veterans: there is no <c>attachInterrupt()</c> here. You now do its three hidden jobs yourself: turn on the source, allow it through the NVIC, and supply the handler under the table's exact name.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>From the pre-class video — ninety seconds, then we program it.</caption>
</slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-interrupt-execution">
                <caption>What an interrupt does.  Hardware pushes the machine
                state onto the stack; the interrupt controller loads the ISR's
                address from the vector table into the PC; the ISR runs; on
                return the state is popped and the next instruction of the
                interrupted program executes.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/slide32_a320de49.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8v-interrupt" ref="fig-interrupt-execution">
    <ul>
        <li>An interrupt is a function call <term>hardware</term> makes: finish the current instruction, push the machine state, jump, run, pop, resume.</li>
        <li>The one new part: where to jump comes from the <term>vector table</term> &#8212; a table in flash holding one handler address per interrupt source.</li>
        <li>The function it lands in is your <term>Interrupt Service Routine</term> &#8212; and it can land between <em>any</em> two instructions, even inside <c>delay_ms()</c>.</li>
        <li>The interrupted code never knows. That is the entire magic: main works; hardware watches.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Video slide — the interrupt is a hardware-invoked function call.</caption>
</slide>
            <p>
                <em>Rule 1: the name is fixed, and it must be exact.</em>  You
                never call an ISR — hardware does, through the vector table —
                so the toolchain must know which of your functions to wire into
                which table slot.  It does this by <em>name</em>: each
                interrupt source has one official handler name, and defining a
                function with exactly that name places it in the table.  Get
                one character wrong and nothing complains — your function is
                simply never wired in.  In class you will look up where these
                names come from rather than taking them on faith.  Notice what
                is <em>absent</em> here: there is no
                <c>register_handler(my_function)</c> call anywhere, and
                nothing to pass a function to at run time — the linkage
                happens once, at build time, when the linker matches your
                function's name to the table slot.  For the
                same reason, an ISR takes no arguments and returns nothing:
                hardware, not your code, is the caller, and it has nothing to
                pass and nowhere to put a result.
            </p>
            <p>
                Between the peripherals and the CPU sits a referee: the
                <term>Nested Vectored Interrupt Controller</term> (NVIC), shown
                in <xref ref="fig-nvic-block"/>.  Every interrupt request
                (IRQ) — from timers, UARTs, GPIO pins, the ADC — arrives at
                the NVIC, which decides whether and when the CPU sees it.  Two
                of its jobs matter to us now.  First, enabling: each interrupt
                source has an enable switch in the NVIC, off by default, so no
                peripheral can interrupt until you allow it.  Second, ordering:
                if two interrupts arrive together, the NVIC services the higher
                priority one first.  The Cortex-M0+ provides four priority
                levels; this course leaves every source at its reset default of
                priority 0, so simultaneous interrupts are resolved by IRQ
                number, not priority.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-nvic-block">
                <caption>The NVIC sits between interrupt sources and the
                processor core.  Peripheral interrupt requests (IRQs), GPIO
                lines routed through the EXTI controller, and the core's own
                exceptions all arrive at the NVIC, which forwards them to the
                CPU in priority order.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/nvic_block_diagram.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8v-nvic" ref="fig-nvic-block">
    <ul>
        <li>Between every interrupt source and the CPU sits a referee: the <term>NVIC</term> &#8212; Nested Vectored Interrupt Controller.</li>
        <li>Job one, enabling: every source has a switch in the NVIC, <term>off by default</term>. Nothing interrupts until you allow it.</li>
        <li>Job two, ordering: two requests at once are served by priority &#8212; and this course leaves every source at the same default.</li>
        <li>So on Day 8 you will flip exactly two switches: one in the timer, one in the NVIC.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Video slide — the NVIC referees every interrupt on the chip.</caption>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timers-ref-stacked">
            <title>What Gets Stacked</title>
            <p>
                The pre-class video describes interrupt entry as hardware saving
                <q>the machine state.</q>  The exact contents, for readers who
                want them: on the Cortex-M0+, exception entry pushes a fixed
                eight-word frame — R0<ndash/>R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, 32
                bytes — onto the stack, automatically and unconditionally,
                with no compiler involvement (<em>Arm Cortex-M0+ Devices
                Generic User Guide</em>, <q>Exception entry and return</q>).
                That fixed, hardware-mandated frame is what distinguishes an
                interrupt from an ordinary function call, where the
                <em>compiler</em> decides which registers the function's own
                entry code must save.  Any other registers an ISR uses are
                saved by the ISR's own compiler-generated code, exactly as in
                any function.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timers-ref-isr">
            <title>Writing an ISR: The Rules, and <c>volatile</c></title>
            <p>
                An Interrupt Service Routine looks like an ordinary C
                function, but three rules set it apart, each a consequence of
                the mechanism in <xref ref="subsec-timers-ref-mechanism"/>.
                Rule 1 — the fixed, exact name, and no arguments or return
                value — is covered there.  The other two:
            </p>
                        <slide xml:id="sl-day8v-isr-rules">
    <p><term>Three rules for the function the hardware calls.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><term>1 &#8212; The name is fixed, and must be exact.</term> Hardware finds your ISR through the vector table, wired by name at build time. There is no <c>register_handler(my_function)</c> call &#8212; and no arguments, no return value, because hardware is the caller.</li>
        <li><term>2 &#8212; Get in, get out.</term> While your ISR runs, main is suspended and other interrupts can be kept waiting. Never <c>printf()</c>, never <c>delay_ms()</c> inside.</li>
        <li><term>3 &#8212; Do the minimum:</term> record what happened &#8212; usually by setting a flag &#8212; and let <c>main()</c> do the real work at its leisure.</li>
        <li>Rule 3's flag has a catch, and it is the next slide.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Video slide — the ISR rules.</caption>
</slide>

<p>
                <em>Rule 2: get in and out fast, and never block.</em>  While
                your ISR runs, the interrupted program is stopped, and other
                interrupts may be kept waiting.  An ISR that loops, waits on a
                flag, or calls something slow — <c>printf()</c>,
                <c>delay_ms()</c> — holds up everything below it.  The
                standard pattern: do the minimum the event requires (clear the
                flag, record a value, set a marker) and let <c>main()</c> do
                the real work at its leisure.
            </p>
            <p>
                <em>Rule 3: shared variables must be <c>volatile</c>.</em>
                The minimal-work pattern of Rule 2 usually means the ISR sets
                a global flag variable that <c>main()</c> checks:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
volatile int timerElapsed;   // shared between the ISR and main

int main(void) {
    timerElapsed = 0;
    while (1) {
        if (timerElapsed) {
            timerElapsed = 0;     // consume the flag ...
            // ... then do the work the event calls for
        } else {
            // free to do something else
        }
    }
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The keyword <c>volatile</c> is what makes this work — and to
                see why it is needed, you need one fact about how C is built,
                a fact no interpreted language prepares you for.  C is not
                executed line by line as written: the compiler is free to rewrite your code into
                anything that behaves identically <em>for everything it can
                see</em>, including deleting a read, or a whole branch, that
                it can prove makes no difference.  Now apply that to the loop
                above (<xref ref="fig-compiler-view"/>): inside
                <c>main()</c>, nothing ever sets <c>timerElapsed</c>, so an
                optimizing compiler is entitled to conclude the flag is
                always 0, read it once (or never), and quietly turn
                <c>if (timerElapsed)</c> into <c>if (0)</c>.  The ISR's write
                happens outside the compiler's view of that loop.  Declaring
                the variable <c>volatile</c> tells the compiler: this value
                can change at any time, by means you cannot see — fetch it
                fresh from memory at every use.  Every variable an ISR shares
                with the rest of the program gets <c>volatile</c>, always.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-compiler-view">
                <caption>Why the flag must be <c>volatile</c>.  When the
                compiler builds <c>main()</c>, its view (dashed boundary)
                contains no write to <c>timerElapsed</c>, so it may legally
                assume the flag never changes.  The ISR's write arrives from
                outside that view, at a moment hardware chooses.
                <c>volatile</c> forbids the assumption and forces a fresh
                read at every use.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day08-Interrupts/compiler_view_volatile.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8-flag" ref="fig-compiler-view">
    <p><term>Step 3 of 3: how the ISR talks to main — the flag, resurfaced.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
volatile int timerElapsed;    // volatile: "fetch it fresh, at every use"

// the ISR:   timerElapsed = 1;                       <- ALL the work it does
// the loop:  if (timerElapsed) { timerElapsed = 0;  ...do the real work... }
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Why <c>volatile</c>: inside the loop, nothing ever sets the flag &#8212; so the optimizer may read it once and turn <c>if (timerElapsed)</c> into <c>if (0)</c>. The ISR's write happens outside the compiler's view.</li>
        <li>Without it: no error, no warning &#8212; the blink just never happens.</li>
        <li>ISR rules, one line each: get in, set the flag, get out. Never <c>printf()</c>, never <c>delay_ms()</c>.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day8v-volatile" ref="fig-compiler-view">
    <ul>
        <li>C is compiled, not read line-by-line: the compiler may legally rewrite your code into anything that behaves the same <em>for everything it can see</em>.</li>
        <li>Inside <c>main()</c>'s loop, nothing ever sets the flag &#8212; so the optimizer may read it once and decide it is 0 forever. The ISR's write happens <term>outside the compiler's view</term>.</li>
        <li>The keyword <c>volatile</c> on the declaration forbids that assumption: fetch it fresh from memory, every use.</li>
        <li><term>Every variable an ISR shares with the rest of the program is volatile. Always.</term></li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Video slide — why the shared flag must be volatile.</caption>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-timers-ref-family">
            <title>The Rest of the Timer Family</title>
            <p>
                The STM32C031C6's five timers — TIM1, TIM3, TIM14, TIM16,
                TIM17 — share the counter/prescaler/auto-reload core this
                chapter taught, and differ in what is bolted around it.  TIM14
                is the simplest.  TIM16 and TIM17 add more capable
                compare/capture machinery, TIM3 adds four independent channels,
                and TIM1 adds motor-control features.  Two abilities the
                fancier machinery enables, both returning later in the course:
                <term>output compare</term> can drive a pin high and low at
                programmed counts with no CPU involvement — that is PWM, the
                heart of the motor chapters — and <term>input capture</term>
                can record the counter's value at the instant an input pin
                changes, which is how the tachometer will measure speed.  In
                the vector table (RM0490 §11.3, Table 40), TIM14 is position
                19, TIM16 is 21, and TIM17 is 22; TIM1 has two vectors (13
                and 14), and TIM3 has one (16).
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
